---
functor: 1
title: Meaning and style
---

set heading(numbering: none)

Put meaning in the source and style in one rule. This is the most important guideline of Functor.

# The four layers

| Layer | You write | Example |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Meaning | what the object is | `object vec: F` |
| Style | how this kind looks | `set vec(style: arrow)` |
| Construction | the recipe | `F.arrow` |
| Glyph | nothing: the compiler picks or builds it | font glyph or built glyph |

# Vectors

```fn
object vec: F, v
set vec(style: arrow)

$F = m frac(dd v, dd t)$
```

Change `arrow` to `bold`, and every vector in the document changes. You do not edit each formula. `vec(x)` marks one symbol as a vector without a declaration.

# Why not write the operation each time

`F.arrow` in each place is construction in the source. When you want bold vectors later, you must change each one. With `object vec: F` the source says what F is, and one rule says how all vectors look.

The notation check gives a warning when you mix the two: `F.bold` by hand on a declared vector, `F.arrow` when the rule already gives the arrow, or `u.arrow` in one place and `u.bold` in another. The editor inspector shows the four layers of the symbol at the cursor. See [Notation check and trace](help/notation.html).

The same idea works for structures: `mat(...)` is a matrix, and `set mat(fence: brack)` gives every matrix square brackets. See [Grids, matrices, vectors, cases](structures/grids.html).

# Style operations

`bold`, `bb`, `frak`, `script` and `up` change the alphabet of a letter. Use them for fixed notation: `R.bb` for the real numbers, `g.frak` for a Lie algebra, `d.up` for the differential. For a kind of object that can change its look, use `object` and a `set` rule.

# Arrow kinds

`mono`, `epi` and `incl` say what a map is: a monomorphism, an epimorphism, an inclusion. A rule picks the arrow for each kind.

```fn
set mono(style: hook)

$f: A mono B$, $p: B epi C$, $i: U incl X$
```

| Style | Arrow |
|:--|:-:|
| `tail` | $mono$ |
| `twohead` | $epi$ |
| `hook` | $incl$ |
| `plain` | $to$ |

The defaults are `tail` for `mono`, `twohead` for `epi` and `hook` for `incl`. The rules also work in a diagram: `diagram(A, B | f: A mono B)`.

# Greek letters in the whole file

Some authors use ϕ for each φ, or an upright π. Write the plain name in the source, and one rule for the look.

```fn
set greek(phi: alt, epsilon: alt, pi: up)

$phi(x) = epsilon pi$
```

The value is `alt` (the other common form), `up` (upright) or `bold`. `alt` works on `epsilon`, `theta`, `kappa`, `phi`, `rho` and `pi`. A style operation in one place wins over the rule: `phi.up` stays upright.

# Other forms of a symbol

Some symbols have more than one common form: ≤ or ⩽, a short or a long ⇒, an upright or an italic ∂. Write the plain name in the source, and one rule for the look.

```fn
set le(style: alt)
set ge(style: alt)
set implies(style: wide)

$0 le x le 1 implies x^2 le x$, $y ge 0$
```

The target is any built-in symbol name. The value is a short recipe of one to three operations that keep the meaning: `alt`, `bold`, `small`, `tall`, `wide`, `bend`, `slant` and `taper`. An operation that changes the meaning, such as `not` or `hat`, is not a style, so the rule refuses it.

| Rule | Look | Use |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| `set le(style: alt)` | $le.alt$ | the slanted form, common in French and Russian texts |
| `set ge(style: alt)` | $ge.alt$ | the slanted form, to match `le` |
| `set partial(style: slant)` | $partial.slant$ | an italic ∂, to match italic letters |
| `set nabla(style: slant)` | $nabla.slant$ | an italic ∇ |
| `set implies(style: wide)` | $implies.wide$ | a long ⟹, for implications between statements |
| `set iff(style: wide)` | $iff.wide$ | a long ⟺ |
| `set mapsto(style: wide)` | $mapsto.wide$ | a long ⟼ |

Operations in one place add to the rule. With `set le(style: alt)`, `le.not` gives $le.alt.not$. An operation of the same family in one place wins: with `set implies(style: wide)`, `implies.tall` is not made wide too.

`mono`, `epi` and `incl` keep their own arrow styles, and Greek letters use `set greek(...)`.

# Own kinds

`kind` comes in phase 1. Then you can declare a kind, for example `kind tensor`, and give it a style rule.
